#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840} :activity/id #uuid "6fb1a6a6-6d39-5ea2-af30-b6e13fa3c1ac" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134840 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135116 :tutorial/content "When considering an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ as a linear transformation\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^n \\xrightarrow{\\quad A \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^m,$$\n\nthere are **four fundamental subspaces** that naturally occur. The first two are as follows:\n\n- The **column space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ This is the set of all linear combinations of the columns of $A.$\n- The **null space** of $A,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ This is the set of all vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that, when pre-multiplied by $A,$ result in the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}.$\n\nNow, notice that the transpose $A^T$ is an $n \\times m$ matrix and can be considered as the transformation\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^m \\xrightarrow{\\quad A^T \\quad} \\mathbb{R}^n.$$\n\nSo, we have similar subspaces for the transpose $A^T{:}$\n\n- The column space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Col}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ We call this the **row space** of $A$ since the span of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to the span of the rows of $A.$\n- The null space of $A^T,$ denoted $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^m.$ We call this the **left null space** of $A$ since it is equivalent to the set of all vectors $\\mathbf y\\in\\mathbb R^m$ such that $\\mathbf y^T A = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\nLet's discuss how these spaces relate to each other:\n\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ that are orthogonal complements of each other: $$\\text{Col}(A^T) = \\text{Null}(A)^\\perp$$\n- We have two subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ that are orthogonal complements of each other: $$\\text{Col}(A) = \\text{Null}(A^T)^\\perp$$\n\nThe four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ce/ced4191955b6886491bf612c62344d2faf6e7fe8d49884446de0eb86851d22e8.png)\n\nTo see why $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Null}(A^T),$ note the following:\n\n- When calculating the product $A^T \\mathbf x,$ we multiply each row of $A^T$ by $\\mathbf x.$\n- Suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in \\text{Null}(A^T).$ Then, by definition, $A^T \\mathbf x = \\mathbf 0.$\n- Thus, $\\mathbf x$ must be orthogonal to each row of $A^T.$ Equivalently, this means that $\\mathbf x$ is orthogonal to each *column* of $A.$\n- Hence, $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nWe can deduce that $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ by similar reasoning." :tutorial/id #uuid "2b6f45b1-7cfa-5c62-9928-caeff36d1cc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5443 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "f59f0795-5e5a-517e-8142-7251b3810d58" :step/math-academy-id 33903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133891 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133707 :question/id #uuid "47945f1d-ab72-5fe8-bc2c-43a69480b323" :question/math-academy-id "e-9090"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ada5d20-89d8-5bc9-9b10-172356be0eb4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050662} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050733}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134083} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362723} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380572} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407278}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Orthogonal Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "a063cde8-8349-5bde-8239-ea3b6c3146eb" :step/math-academy-id 33904 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133709 :question/id #uuid "bb597435-f43e-54de-a219-1af646efcb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-9099"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1a36c7f7-4287-562b-94ea-047b38ede70f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051002}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380586} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407276}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "f05c128d-c2b3-55a6-b5af-aa01107d4052" :step/math-academy-id 33918 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133710 :question/id #uuid "bd1f48b4-8bfc-536d-8145-0effcf44a788" :question/math-academy-id "e-9104"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e1da77-a5dc-5f78-977e-0d6dae99c97e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049545}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407279}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Images of the Four Fundamental Subspaces of the Matrix Under Its Action"} :step/id #uuid "f70134b1-0b93-5597-a6b6-16fdc7fb1bad" :step/math-academy-id 33937 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135117 :tutorial/content "Given an $m \\times n$ matrix $A,$ what is the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under $A?$\n\nNote that pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ by $A$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A{:}$ $$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & ⋯ & {a}_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large A} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\mathbf{x}} = x_1 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] + x_2 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] +\\cdots+ x_n [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{n} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTherefore, by definition, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ the column space of $A.$\n\n![](images/db/dbefb436195af5ffa8841f4119054426db50ac2987138a5912991cf42321e0dc.png)\n\nSimilarly, pre-multiplying a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^m$ by $A^T$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $A^T{:}$ $$\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large A^T} \\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{aligned}]}_{\\large \\mathbf{x}} = x_1 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] + x_2 [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}] +\\cdots+ x_m [\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathbb{R}^m$ under the action of $A^T$ must be $\\text{Col}(A^T).$ This is also the row space of $A,$ since a linear combination of the columns of $A^T$ is equivalent to a linear combination of the rows of $A.$\n\n![](images/c8/c82ab88289fc2d35ce354933937d908c54a9839bfa0fd126187a9723c58eeb27.png)\n\nWe can use similar reasoning to find the image of the four fundamental subspaces under the appropriate matrix." :tutorial/id #uuid "915420e9-f241-5e99-8274-32ffdfb79b9a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5454 :tutorial/title "Images Under the Action of a Matrix and Its Transpose"} :step/id #uuid "e8b74e81-7593-5f0a-9136-22383330a688" :step/math-academy-id 33950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134844}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134846 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135118 :tutorial/content "Consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ with rank $r.$ The dimensions of the four fundamental subspaces of $A$ are as follows:\n\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A)) = r.$ Since the column space is the span of the columns of $A,$ its dimension is the number of linearly independent columns which, by Gaussian elimination, is $\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T)) = r.$ It can be shown that the dimension of the row space of $A$ also equals $r.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(A^T)=\\text{rank}(A)=r.$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = n-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we have that $$\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^n)}_{n} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A^T))}_{r} = n-r.$$\n- $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = m-r.$ Since $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ is the orthogonal complement of $\\text{Col}(A)$ in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ we have that $$\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A^T)) = \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\mathbb{R}^m)}_{m} - \\underbrace{\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}(A))}_{r} = m-r.$$\n\nLet's add these dimensions to the diagram below.\n\n![](images/1c/1c550b87048bba6a7c362f00f09faca74ca9b056a93698436d0994c120971356.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "40af35b2-abb6-59b8-999a-9976edbb9ce8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5455 :tutorial/title "Dimensions of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "957cde37-32d4-515e-ad96-75c96f7bfa57" :step/math-academy-id 33951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134843}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134847 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135119 :tutorial/content "Let's review what we have learned in this lesson.\n\nIf we consider an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ of rank $r$ as a transformation from $\\mathbb{R}^n$ to $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ then there are four fundamental subspaces of $A{:}$\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^m$ with dimensions $r$ and $m-r,$ respectively.\n- $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ and $\\text{Null}(A)$ are subspaces in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ with dimensions $r$ and $n-r,$ respectively.\n\nFurthermore,\n\n- $\\text{Col}(A)$ and $\\text{Null}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^m,$ and\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$ and $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ are orthogonal complements in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A^T)$ under the action of $A$ is $\\text{Col}(A),$ and\n- the image of $\\text{Col}(A)$ under the action of $A^T$ is $\\text{Col}(A^T).$\n\nThese facts about the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/37/37f633d0bca1a599f7d1658c051f802da5d607d420beeb4bddbef5cd98c366ef.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "190e1777-4f27-5c94-88bd-21c9992eb024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5456 :tutorial/title "The Big Picture of the Four Fundamental Subspaces"} :step/id #uuid "8eef352e-273a-5614-b97d-8c357807e9a0" :step/math-academy-id 33952}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186134047}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046679} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407280} :activity/id #uuid "d12b0c27-35c4-517e-8eb3-41dab352b8fa" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887 :topic/id #uuid "1aba1eae-fe85-559f-b4fb-b8896b64623f" :topic/math-academy-id 2200} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61b8af3e-3b47-5aa2-91bd-6b1c4c03c696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407251} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b64355-3c4a-5468-b79c-60409857f476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407255} {:answer/id #uuid "bc41a449-214b-5c1b-8a85-1c8dc86ad746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407259} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e3cd61-e48f-5faf-ad9f-c294bdd8ce47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407268} {:answer/id #uuid "f62c90f5-3a7e-558e-82d7-d3e2ac3e06e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407269}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407259} :answer-field/id #uuid "65c8f326-9093-58bd-a6e1-bf36f4cf4e21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9f78c95-881f-537a-b5e6-305264553498" :question/math-academy-id "q-109777" :question/problem "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Col}(A)$ for every matrix $A?$\n\n- $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$\n- $\\text{Null}(A)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ consists of all linear combinations of the columns of $A$, that is, the rows of ${A}^{T}.$\n\nIf a vector is orthogonal to every linear combination of the rows of ${A}^{T},$ it must be orthogonal to each row of ${A}^{T}.$\nBut notice that when pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$ by that vector.\nSo, $\\text{Col}(A)$ must be orthogonal to the left null space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/de/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)"} :step/id #uuid "453b68d4-1d9d-50c9-a90c-51c8b99946a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407282}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ad86372-161b-56b5-b9b3-67cb9855544f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407248} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4b4700-8836-57b3-a254-6685beac57ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407249} {:answer/id #uuid "65001b28-0435-5b9d-91ec-614c84c822e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407253} {:answer/id #uuid "bf467989-30ff-5c80-94cc-972d1936e437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407260} {:answer/id #uuid "d63461e1-95f1-5efd-aec4-a9f50c64386c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "{R}^{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407264}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407260} :answer-field/id #uuid "32cd5975-d7c1-5dd8-91de-4834a553c50c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b9c49e80-5070-56aa-b070-99b1fa2fdff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-109848" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $m\\times n$ matrix. What is the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${A}^{T}$ is a $n\\times m$ matrix.\n\nPre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{m}$ by ${A}^{T}$ gives a linear combination of the columns of ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} & {a}_{2}^{T} & ⋯ & {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{A}^{T}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{m}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{1}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{2}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+⋯+{x}_{m}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ {a}_{m}^{T} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{m}$ under the action of ${A}^{T}$ must be $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}),$ the row space of $A.$"} :step/id #uuid "6ef8f97d-5b2a-5ab0-998f-92729ef9dc31" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407284}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407282 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407275 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "166f24b3-2b33-5589-9ba5-efd73efdf696" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407247} {:answer/id #uuid "794d7c6a-4727-5c98-8506-efa641e24334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407254} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f31e2f-2172-581c-b46f-338027d98c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407261} {:answer/id #uuid "de4a68e5-c938-5191-8ef4-522e158bedb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407265} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d32fbe-6d2e-5aca-9070-ed1ab0efc4fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407261} :answer-field/id #uuid "077cd26a-5956-5d07-8539-e101ab53d7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d75070f-88dd-5b20-8fec-c1f3c6de013c" :question/math-academy-id "q-109846" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 4$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=3,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=5,$ $n=4,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(A)) & =n-r=4-3=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6f121dc0-6e9f-506e-8370-6a73e7b21516" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407283 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407276 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e0fd37b-833c-5581-bf94-abc93beffe65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407246} {:answer/id #uuid "6203c04e-a6ad-568e-b086-f63c9a0600d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407252} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ba999d-5dc0-5e42-af53-78dc73ee4de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407256} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9ba337-a858-55b1-bf28-9f915ba4dcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407258} {:answer/id #uuid "e18de25a-171d-5acb-88a3-6812297610fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407266}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407258} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82e089a-caba-5439-8d15-a646e7c8e9fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "66ca173a-d952-58d1-b484-502590c17693" :question/math-academy-id "q-109873" :question/problem "Consider a $5\\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\text{dim}(\\text{Col}({A}^{T}))=3,$ then $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T}))=$" :question/worked-solution "The four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/889b83fadd26b377c4dd34143c9b16541630c04e90de3d9b005e7147ead75ad5.png)\n\nIn our case, we are given that $m=5,$ $n=6,$ and $r=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}({A}^{T})) & =m-r=5-3=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9114f2aa-68cd-5878-a5bf-ad32cd59fd29"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407284 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407279 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d3a4ec2-bd1c-569f-bc25-a735fbf6e8f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407245} {:answer/id #uuid "4624e774-8f8e-50d7-922a-9f2511cb84dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{Null}(AB)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407250} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8461c5-2db5-555b-98b8-ef3d5aadb4ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{Col}({B}^{T}{A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407257} {:answer/id #uuid "ca1a752d-a1b9-5c75-a6eb-005f23fda936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{Null}({B}^{T}{A}^{T})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407262} {:answer/id #uuid "cafe991c-8437-5728-ad05-fe57c0cef2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "\\text{Col}(AB)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407263}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407263} :answer-field/id #uuid "a14d9fc2-b7d8-5af8-b07b-319f63dc6ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "de769de3-3ab7-5536-ae28-2dcbd744b48f" :question/math-academy-id "q-109861" :question/problem "Let $A$ and $B$ be $m\\times p$ and $p\\times n$ matrices, respectively. What is the image of $\\text{Col}({B}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})$ under the action of $AB?$" :question/worked-solution "Pre-multiplying a vector $x\\in {R}^{n}$ by $AB$ gives a linear combination of the columns of $AB:$\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}| & | & ⋯ & | \\\\ (ab)_{1} & (ab)_{2} & ⋯ & (ab)_{n} \\\\ | & | & ⋯ & |\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{AB}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}={x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ (ab)_{1} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ (ab)_{2} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]+⋯+{x}_{n}[\\begin{aligned}| \\\\ (ab)_{n} \\\\ |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of ${R}^{n}$ under the action of $AB$ must be $\\text{Col}(AB),$ the column space of $AB.$\n\nFurthermore, since $\\text{Null}(AB)$ is mapped to the zero vector $0$ by the definition of the null subspace, the image of its orthogonal complement $\\text{Col}((AB)^{T})=\\text{Col}({B}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})$ must be $\\text{Col}(AB).$\n\nThis can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](images/88/885bf8a3061f93ad400897a7273ffe4157d22dee20b8e4ee7ebb982b1c4319bb.png)"} :step/id #uuid "d8a9f51b-41b7-5b08-9832-d19d696012d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407283}}] :activity/title "The Four Fundamental Subspaces of a Matrix" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407244}]}
